$ is defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}-x+3, & x<2, \\\\ 2, & x\\ge 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen,\n\n${\\int }_{-4}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}."] [#edb/ref 17592186377354 "The function $f(x)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{2}x-1, & x<2, \\\\ x-4, & x\\ge 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen,\n\n${\\int }_{-2}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}."] [#edb/ref 17592186377357 "The function $f(x)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}, & x<2, \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2}x+4, & x\\ge 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen,\n\n${\\int }_{-1}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}."] [#edb/ref 17592186377360 "The function $f(x)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-1, & x<1, \\\\ \\frac{4}{x}, & x\\ge 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen,\n\n${\\int }_{-1}^{{e}^{3}}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}."] [#edb/ref 17592186377893 "According to the Chinese remainder theorem, which of the following systems of congruences has a unique solution modulo $48?$ \n\n$$\n\\text{I.}\\,{\\begin{aligned}x≡4 & (\\text{mod}\\,6) \\\\ x≡5 & (\\text{mod}\\,8)\\end{aligned}\\,\\text{II.}\\,{\\begin{aligned}x≡2 & (\\text{mod}\\,3) \\\\ x≡14 & (\\text{mod}\\,16)\\end{aligned}\\,\\text{III.}\\,{\\begin{aligned}x≡3 & (\\text{mod}\\,4) \\\\ x≡7 & (\\text{mod}\\,12)\\end{aligned}\\,\n$$\n\nSelect all that apply."] [#edb/ref 17592186379425 "Consider the following initial value problem for the functions $y=y(x),z=z(x):$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=x(y-z),\\, & y(0)=-1 \\\\ {z}^{′}=y(z-x),\\, & z(0)=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse Euler's method with two steps of equal size to approximate $y(1)$ and $z(1).$\n\n$y(1)≈$\n{{field-1}},\n$\\,z(1)≈$\n{{field-2}}"] [#edb/ref 17592186379430 "Consider the following initial value problem for the functions $y=y(x),z=z(x):$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}={x}^{2}+3y-z,\\, & y(0)=0 \\\\ {z}^{′}=x+{y}^{2},\\, & z(0)=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse Euler's method with two steps of equal size to approximate $y(2)$ and $z(2).$\n\n$y(2)≈$\n{{field-1}},\n$\\,z(2)≈$\n{{field-2}}"] [#edb/ref 17592186379435 "Consider the following initial value problem for the functions $y=y(x),z=z(x):$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=2x+y+3z,\\, & y(0)=1 \\\\ {z}^{′}=x-y+2z,\\, & z(0)=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using Euler's method.\nWhat entries should be placed in the columns for $Δy$ and $Δz$ in the table below if the step size is $Δx=\\frac{1}{4}?$\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ |  |  | {{field-1}} |  |  | {{field-2}} |\n\nTherefore, $y\\,(\\frac{1}{4})≈$\n{{field-3}}\nand $z\\,(\\frac{1}{4})≈$\n{{field-4}}."] [#edb/ref 17592186379478 "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)={x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=-{x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear ODEs above, where $t\\in R.$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below.$\\,$ The function $a(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}t \\\\ 1-t\\end{bmatrix}$ \n{{field-1}} \na solution of the system.\n\n$\\,$ The function $b(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}{t}^{2}+1 \\\\ -t\\end{bmatrix}$ \n{{field-2}} \na solution of the system.\n\n$\\,$ The function $c(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}t+1 \\\\ -t\\end{bmatrix}$ \n{{field-3}} \na solution of the system.\n\n$\\,$ {{field-4}} \nis a set of fundamental solutions to the system because the Wronskian \n\n$$\nW({{field-5}})={{field-6}}\\ne 0\n$$\n\n$\\,$ for at least one $t\\in R.$"] [#edb/ref 17592186379502 "Consider the following initial value problem for the functions $y=y(x),z=z(x),w=w(x):$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=2x+2y-z,\\, & y(0)=1 \\\\ {z}^{′}=x+y+3z,\\, & z(0)=0 \\\\ {w}^{′}=wy+2xz,\\, & w(0)=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using Euler's method.\nWhat entries should be placed in the columns for $Δy,$ $Δz,$ and $Δw$ in the table below if the step size is $Δx=1?$\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ | $w$ | ${w}^{′}$ | $Δw$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | 