us random variable $X$ has the probability density function $f(x)$ stated below. Given that $\\text{E}[X]=4$ compute $\\text{Var}[X].$ \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2x}{27}, & 0<x<3, \\\\ \\frac{9-x}{27}, & 3\\le x<9, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186486383 "Find $\\text{E}[\\frac{1}{XY}],$ given that random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{4}{x}^{2}{y}^{3},\\, & 0<x<1,\\,0<y<2, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186486393 "Suppose we have a random sample of size $n=140$ from a population where $60%$ of the population has a particular characteristic. Let the random variable ${X}_{i}$ be equal to\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=\\{\\begin{aligned}1,\\,i\\text{th member of the sample has the characteristic} \\\\ 0,\\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $i=1,…,140.$ If the statistic $X$ is defined as\n\n$$\nX={\\sum }_{i=1}^{140}{X}_{i}\n$$\n\nfind approximate values of $\\text{E}[X]$ and $\\text{Var}[X].$\n\n*Hint: You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.*"] [#edb/ref 17592186486412 "Find $\\text{E}[X+3{Y}^{2}],$ given that random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{15},\\, & 0\\le x\\le 3,\\,\\frac{x}{3}\\le y\\le 3; \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186486430 "Find $\\text{E}[XY],$ given that random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3xy},\\, & 1\\le x\\le y,\\,e\\le y\\le {e}^{2}, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186486435 "Suppose we have a random sample of size $n=66$ from a population where $42%$ of the population has a particular characteristic. Let the random variable ${X}_{i}$ be equal to\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=\\{\\begin{aligned}1,\\,i\\text{th member of the sample has the characteristic} \\\\ 0,\\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $i=1,…,66.$ Find an approximate sampling distribution of the statistic $X={\\sum }_{i=1}^{66}{X}_{i}.$\n\n*Hint: You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.*"] [#edb/ref 17592186491208 "The random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}cx{y}^{2},\\, & 0\\le y\\le 1,\\,y\\le x\\le 2-y, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $c$ is a positive real number. Find the value of $c.$"] [#edb/ref 17592186492840 "Consider the following system of equations\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned} & x-4y=-1 \\\\  & 2x-7y=0 \\\\  & -4x+15y=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The system is consistent independent\n\nII. The system has no solution\n\nIII. The system is consistent dependent"] [#edb/ref 17592186492874 "Determine the value of $k$ for which the following system of linear equations is inconsistent:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned} & 2x-y=3 \\\\  & -4x+(k-5)y=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186493914 "Suppose we would like to find the minimum value of the function $f(x,y,z)=xy+{z}^{2}$ on the intersection of the surfaces ${z}^{2}+{x}^{2}=4$ and $x+2y=-2$ using the method of Lagrange multipliers. Then, the corresponding Lagrange multipliers $\\lambda $ and $\\mu $ satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}⟨y,\\,x,\\,2z⟩=\\lambda ⟨\\,\\bbox[2pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AA}},\\,0,\\,2z⟩+\\mu ⟨1,\\,\\bbox[2pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AA}},\\,0⟩ \\\\ {z}^{2}+{x}^{2}=4 \\\\ x+2y=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom left to right, what are the missing expressions?"] [#edb/ref 17592186493939 "Suppose we would like to find the maximum of the function $f(x,y,z)=3x+2z+{y}^{2}$ on the intersection of the surfaces ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1$ and $2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+4{z}^{2}=1$ using the method of Lagrange multipliers. Then, the corresponding Lagrange multipliers $\